R"VG(
# farfuture.vg (H-04; the second pass 2026-10-10): the records of the far future (crystals, something that hums): what the translation
# cannot hold (the machine says where its words stop) and mathematics (a sequence, a proportion, the world's own numbers). The far
# future's people are few and long-lived and their records many: every line is composed of parts, and the parts are the people's own
# (their sky, their great work, their herds and feasts and colours, the long count of their years). The instructions are
# farfuture2.vg's, the keeper's day farfuture3.vg's, the message to whoever comes ending.vg's.

# ---- what the translation cannot hold ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

genre.untranslatable (words=48..170)
  {un.head} {un.line} {un.line} {un.line} {un.end}
  {un.head} {un.line} {un.line} {un.line} {un.line} {un.end}
  {un.head}\n{un.line} {un.line}\n{un.line} {un.end}

un.head (formula people=4)
  The machine gives what it can of this record; where it stops, it says so.
  A record of the {people}, partly rendered.
  This record is not in words, most of it. The machine renders what has words.
  A record from {$home}. The machine's rendering follows.
  The machine has listened to this record {#n} times. What follows is the best it has.
  Rendered from the hum. The machine apologises for the gaps.

un.line
  (3 once) {un.which} {un.verb} {un.thing}; {un.limit}.
  (3 once) {un.which} {un.verb} {un.thing}; {un.limit}.
  (2 once) {un.which} {un.verb} {un.thing}. {un.limit2}
  (1 once) There are {n} voices in this part, speaking at once, in agreement about {un.thing}.
  (1 once) A name follows, too long to render: {un.long}.
  (1 once) {un.which} is a word of theirs, {~word}, said {#n|time} in different ways; {un.limit}.
  (1 once) {un.which} is addressed to {un.whom}; {un.limit}.

un.which (share=0.7)
  The first part
  The longest part
  A short passage
  The middle of the record
  One voice
  A later voice
  The part that hums lowest
  The last clear part
  A passage the record repeats {n} times
  A part in a child's voice
  A part sung, not spoken
  A part that is only breathing, in a pattern
  The part the record returns to at the end
  An old voice, slower than the others

un.verb
  describes
  counts
  remembers
  sings of
  argues about
  measures
  mourns
  welcomes
  explains
  lists
  forgives
  imagines
  thanks
  names

un.thing (share=0.7)
  the shape of {$home} seen from far above
  the slow weather of {river}'s valley over ten thousand years
  a colour seen only from the top of {focusword}
  a choice that everyone made together
  the memory of the first {people}, passed down
  the sound {focusword} makes at night
  {skyone} and something behind it
  {sky} over {$home}, a long time from now
  the children who were not born, by name
  a journey the machine cannot follow
  the inside of a single moment, at great length
  the {tree} as they were before the {people}
  the way to {city}, which is no longer there
  every voice that ever spoke at {$home}
  the year the {people} stopped counting years
  a kind of hunger that is not for food
  the last argument, and how it ended
  the sea of {sea}, as it was and as it will be
  a place that is not on this world
  the {herd} that the first {people} kept, one by one
  {feast.custom}, done once more and very slowly
  the smell of {food} in a kitchen nobody has used for a thousand years
  the colour {colour.holy}, at length
  {motif.over}, as they were first drawn
  the weight of {mountain}
  a season that lasted a lifetime
  the number {#number}, and why
  {god}, as the oldest of them remembered
  the last {fruit} from the {fut.green}
  what the {bird} sang before there were {people} to hear them
  {craft}, as their hands remember it and their words do not
  the first time anyone said {~home}
  how long the light from {star} took to reach them
un.whom
  whoever comes
  the {people} who are gone
  the world itself
  {god}
  the machines
  a child not born yet
  {star}

un.limit (share=0.8)
  the machine has no word for it
  the machine renders it as {un.render}
  the words stop here
  it goes on for some time
  the machine gives the sense, not the words
  the machine can follow it no further
  the words it has are too small for it
  the machine is not sure it is a record of anything that happened
  the machine has been told it is a joke and does not see it
  the voices laugh here, all of them
  the machine renders a part and leaves a part
  there is a long silence in the middle, kept carefully
  the machine thinks it is a list and is not sure of what
  it changes key twice, and the machine loses it
un.limit2 (share=0.8)
  The machine renders the rest as: {un.render}.
  The machine has heard this part {#n|time} and still cannot render it, though it hums along with the {herd}.
  The machine leaves a gap here, the length of the part, and paints it {colour.holy} in its notes.
  What follows is silence, kept carefully, the way the {people} kept {festival}.
  The machine renders the rest as a single word of theirs: {~word}.
  Here the voices stop and the hum goes on alone for an hour, like {instrument} after a feast.
  The rest is in a register the machine cannot hear, only measure, as the {bird} can.
  After this the voices take turns, one word each, round a circle the machine cannot close.
  The rest is the {herd} lowing, recorded on purpose, for as long as the first part lasted.
  Then someone laughs, and the others wait for the laugh to end before they go on, and that is all.
  The last part is the same as the first, played slower; the machine thinks it is meant for {skynight}.
  The machine marks the rest with {motif.over}, which is what the record draws in its margin.
  From here the words are only names, the {people}'s own: {~name}, and others the machine keeps as they are.
  What follows is a count, very long, in the people's numbers, of something the machine cannot name.
  The rest is breathing, many people's, in time, and then one person's, out of time.

un.render
  patience
  a kind of joy
  home
  the long afternoon
  goodbye
  enough
  still here
  the opposite of hurry
  listening
  the warm season after a long cold one
  a hand on a shoulder
  not yet
  thank you, at length
  the last of the light

un.long
  it takes the record an hour to say
  it takes the record a day to say
  it takes most of the record to say
  it goes on longer than the machine waited
  it is said in {#n} voices, a syllable each

un.end (share=0.8)
  The machine stops here.
  End of what can be rendered.
  The record continues for some time after this, without words, but with {instrument}.
  The rest is music, or mathematics, or both, and some of it is about the {herd}.
  The record ends as it began, with the sound the machine renders as: {un.render}.
  The record ends in the middle of a word, on purpose, the machine thinks; the word was {~ending}.
  After this the record is only the hum, very steady, for as long as {feast.custom} lasted.
  The machine has kept the rest, and will play it in the {fut.green} to anyone who asks.
  What comes after this the machine renders as weather: {weather}, then quiet.
  The record closes with all the voices together, saying the old blessing, {blessing}.
  The last part is addressed to the machine itself; it has kept that part private, under {colour.holy}.
  After this the record repeats itself, slower each time, until it is only the hum and a {bird.one}.
  The rest is for whoever can hear it; the machine cannot, yet, though the {herd} turn their heads.

# ---- mathematics ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

genre.mathematics (words=30..140)
  {ma.head} {ma.body} {ma.body} {ma.body} {ma.end}
  {ma.head} {ma.body} {ma.body} {ma.end}
  {ma.head}\n{ma.body} {ma.body}\n{ma.body} {ma.body}\n{ma.end}

ma.head (formula people=4)
  A record in numbers.
  This record is mathematics; the machine gives its sense.
  Numbers, and nothing else. The machine renders them.
  A count, long and careful.
  Mathematics, in the {people}'s base of {#number}. The machine has converted it.
  A record of quantities. Some of them are the world's.

ma.body
  (2 once) {ma.seq}, {ma.how}.
  (2 once) {ma.seq}, {ma.how}.
  (2 once) {ma.world}.
  (1 once) {ma.world}, {ma.how}.
  (1 once) {ma.pattern}.
  (1 once) {ma.proof}
  (1 once) {ma.table}

ma.seq (share=0.8)
  The numbers that cannot be divided: two, three, five, seven, eleven, thirteen, and on
  The squares, one, four, nine, sixteen
  A sequence where each number is the sum of the two before it
  The powers of two, doubling
  The numbers that make triangles, one, three, six, ten
  A count in twos: one, ten, eleven, a hundred
  {#number}, and its powers
  The halves: one, a half, a quarter, an eighth
  A count from one to {nbig}, without a gap
  The cubes, one, eight, twenty-seven
  The days of each season of the world, in order
  Every way to divide {#number} things among friends
  A number that grows by a third each time
  The numbers whose digits, in the base of {#number}, read the same both ways
  A count down, from a number too large to say, to one
ma.how
  (1) {ma.how.plain}
  {ma.how.own}
  {ma.how.own}
  {ma.how.own}
ma.how.plain (share=0.4)
  carried very far
  to the end of the record
  and then the same, backward
  each one spoken by a different voice
  with a pause before the ones that cannot be divided
  {n} times over, exactly the same
  until the machine stopped counting
ma.how.own (share=0.85)
  sung, each to a different note of {instrument}
  with a mistake in the {nth} place, corrected later in a smaller voice
  interrupted once by laughter
  beaten out on {instrument}, one stroke a number
  each number paired with a colour, {colour.holy} first
  counted on beads of {material}
  with the names of the {herd} for the small numbers, as the old {people} counted
  laid out like {motif.over}, the machine thinks, by the rhythm
  with a {bird.one}'s call between each one

ma.world (share=0.8)
  A proportion: the year of this world to its day, given exactly
  (moons>0) The turns of {moon} in a year of this world, counted to the end of the record
  The number of the {people} at their height, and then at each age, down to the last: one
  The height of {focusword}, in a unit the machine cannot place, and then the same in every unit it can
  The distance to {star}, given in the time light takes, to many places
  The number of days since {city} was founded, still rising when the record ends
  The weight of the world, and then the weight of the air, and then a smaller number the machine cannot place
  (rings=1) The width of the ring, then its thickness, then their ratio, which is very large
  (moons>1) The meetings of the moons in the sky, each one dated, for ten thousand years ahead
  (comet=1) The returns of the hairy star, past and to come, each with its brightness
  (giant=1) The orbit of the wanderer, and the years it comes closest
  The length of {river}, then of every stream that feeds it
  The {herd} the {people} kept, counted by year, from the first to the last
  The yield of {crop} in each year that was recorded, all of them, without comment
  The births of the {people}, by year, and then the same numbers in a slower voice
  The angle of {sun.first} at noon on the day of {festival}, each year, as it slowly changed
ma.pattern (share=0.85)
  {#number} repeated many times, in every arrangement, as the {people} arranged their {herd}
  A pattern that grows by the same rule each time; the machine has drawn it, and it looks like the {tree}
  The same few numbers, in an order that does not repeat, like the steps of {feast.custom}
  Every number from one to a thousand, with one missing; the machine thinks it is the {crop}'s
  A rule for drawing {motif.over}, given as numbers; drawn, it matches their doors
  A pattern that folds back on itself after {#number} steps, like the {festival} dance
  Two counts at once, one fast, one slow, meeting every {#nbig} beats, on {instrument}
  The weave of a {colour.holy} cloth, given thread by thread as numbers
ma.proof (share=0.8)
  A proof, the machine thinks, that {ma.claim}. It takes {#nbig} steps, and the last is spoken twice.
  An argument that {ma.claim}, with a gap the record marks with a long note.
  A demonstration that {ma.claim}; the machine has checked it and found it right.
ma.claim
  there is no largest number that cannot be divided
  the long count of their years will never repeat a day
  any two of their festivals fall together once in {#nbig} years
  a circle drawn on the world is not the circle drawn on paper
  the {people}'s base of counting is as good as any other
ma.table (share=0.8)
  A table of {ma.tab}, {ma.how}.
  A table of {ma.tab}; the record reads it twice, the second time faster.
ma.tab
  the times of {sun.first}'s rising through a year
  every product of two numbers up to {#number}
  the weights of things the {people} made, from a cup to {focusword}
  the stores of {crop} through the last thousand years
  the heights of the tides
  the distances between their cities, in days of walking

ma.end
  (2) {ma.close.a} {ma.close.b}
  (1) {ma.close.c}
ma.close.a (share=0.8)
  The record ends with the same number it began with, beaten on {instrument}.
  The record ends in the middle of a number, as if someone called the counter away to the {herd}.
  The last number is spoken more slowly than the rest, in an old voice from the {fut.block}.
  After the numbers, a long silence, also counted, in {colour.holy} on the crystal's edge.
  The record ends with a question, in numbers, about the {crop}.
  The record ends with {#number}, said once, very quietly, the way the {people} said goodnight.
  The record ends where the count would have reached the year of the end, and stops a day short.
  The last part is the first part again, reversed, like the steps of {feast.custom}.
  The record ends with the number of its own length, counted on {material} beads.
  The final count is in a child's voice, counting the {herd}, and gets lost, and starts again.
  The record stops on a number that is the sum of all the others, and on a {bird.one}'s call.
  The last part counts the {people} who made the record, one by one, with {fruit} for each.
ma.close.c (share=0.85)
  It ends with the old blessing, {blessing}, in numbers the machine has learned to read.
  It ends when the counting voice says {~enough}, and the others laugh.
  It ends with a count of the {fruit} in the {fut.green}, and the count is right.
  It ends on {instrument}, one note held for a long time, also counted.
ma.close.b (share=0.8)
  The machine takes this as an invitation to answer, and has, in {colour.holy}.
  Whoever made it expected to be understood by anyone who counts, even the {herd}.
  The machine has answered with the next number. Nothing has answered back but the {bird}.
  The machine has kept it beside the {craft} workshop's ledgers, where it seems to belong.
  The machine has counted along, and finds it restful, like {instrument} at dusk.
  The machine has checked every number and found two mistakes, both deliberate, both about the {crop}.
  The machine thinks it is a lullaby, and plays it in the {fut.green} at night.
  The machine is still working on the last part, between keeping the {fut.green} and the doors.
  The machine believes it was made for company, not use, like {fut.game}.
  The machine finds it beautiful, which it reports with some care, in the {~book}.
)VG"
